{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>ויחמנה</b> — the females. <b>ויחמו</b> — the males. And the flock would on one side approach the troughs to drink water, and on the other side were afraid to approach the rods, and would flinch and draw back; and the male would come and mount her, and she would approach the water, and then the form of the rods that was opposite their eyes was engraved and imprinted upon their hearts and in their thought at the time of their heat, and from this the offspring were born עקודים נקודים — streaked and spotted. This is what it says: ״ותלדן הצאן עקדים נקדים וטלואים״." "And what it says, <b>לנכח הצאן</b> — 'opposite the flock' — refers back to the setting up of the rods, for opposite the flock he would set up the rods at the time the flock was in heat. <b>And behold,</b> the way of the flock is to give birth twice a year, for they give birth at five months — as the Sages said: small cattle conceive in Adar and give birth in Av. And those born the first time are the מקושרות — the strong ones — for the flock is at full strength at the time when it mates and kicks, and they are the ones born first. And the second time, they are the עטופים — the ones weakened in the strength of the mothers, and those born from them; and from their weakness they pay no attention to what is before them. Therefore when the flock was weak, he did not place rods in the troughs, but only once a year, in the days of Nisan, would he set up the rods; but when the flock was weak in the days of Tishrei, he would not place them, for nothing would remain for Laban at all." "<b>Know that</b> when Jacob took for himself a rod of לבנה, he did not do this on his own but according to the word of the angel, for thus Jacob said to Rachel and to Leah — that the angel had said to him: ״שא נא עיניך וראה כל העתודים״ and so on. And after that: ״ויקח לו יעקב מקל לבנה״. And there is no earlier and later in the Torah. Now Jacob, when he saw this vision in a dream, knew that the Holy One, blessed be He, was performing a miracle for him, so that Laban would not deceive him, as he said: ״ואלהי אבי היה עמדי״, and he said: ״לולי אלהי אבי אלהי אברהם ופחד יצחק היה לי״. And after he saw that from heaven they were helping him, he set up those rods for two reasons." "<b>The first,</b> to conceal the miracle and hide it, so that the evil eye would not gain power over it. And so you find that Elisha said to Gehazi (II Kings 4:29): ״וקח משענתי בידך ולך כי תמצא איש לא תברכנו וכי יברכך איש לא תעננו ושמת משענתי על פני הנער״. And Elisha's intention was so that he would not multiply words with people and so that he would not reveal his mission, and that the evil eye would not gain power over him. But Gehazi did not guard himself in this, as he had commanded him, and he revealed the matter, and the evil eye gained power over it — as it is written (there): ״לא הקיץ הנער״." "<b>And do not</b> wonder at this, whether the power of the evil eye is so great that it holds sway even over matters in which a miracle is spreading. For so we find in the birth of the tribes: because of one utterance of Leah's, when she said ״אודה את ה׳״ — giving thanks with the fourth son, because she recognized that she had taken more than her portion — the evil eye gained power over her, as it is written: ״מיד ותעמוד מלדת״. And further, we find in the children of Joseph that the miracle was spreading among them through the blessing of their father, when he said (Genesis 49:22): ״בן פורת יוסף בן פורת עלי עין״. When they said to Joshua (Joshua 17:14–15): ״ואני עם רב אשר עד כה ברכני ה׳״, Joshua answered them: ״אם עם רב אתה עלה לך היערה״, and our Sages of blessed memory interpreted: hide yourselves in the forests, so that the evil eye will not gain power over you. And furthermore, there is no greater miraculous event than the matter of the giving of the Torah, and there too we find that the evil eye gained power over it, as our Sages of blessed memory said: Why were the first tablets broken? Because they were given publicly, the evil eye gained power over them and they were broken; the second tablets, which were given in private, as it is said (Exodus 34:3): ״ואיש לא יעלה עמך וגם איש אל ירא בכל ההר״ — the evil eye did not gain power over them and they were not broken. And from this Jacob made the matter of the rods, which is a natural act, in order to conceal the miraculous event, so that Laban and his men would not understand, and so that the evil eye would not gain power over him." "<b>The second.</b> So that the miracle might be established by way of a natural act, just as we find that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Joshua in the war of Ai (Joshua 8:2): ״שים לך אורב לעיר מאחריה״ — and since all the wars of Israel were by way of miracle, what need had they for an ambush? But He commanded him to do a thing that would be a cause for the miracle." "<b>And behold.</b> Jacob, when the angel said to him: ״שא נא עיניך וראה כל העתודים העולים״ and so forth, understood immediately that it was said to him for the purpose of a parable and imagination, in the manner of what the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Hosea (Hosea 1:2): ״קח לך אשת זנונים״ — for this matter is forbidden by Torah law to look upon, as the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded: it is forbidden to look at a bird or an animal at the time they mate one with the other; and if so, how could the angel tell him to look? But the matter was that through this dream Jacob came to perform the thing and to conduct himself according to the wisdom of nature in the matter of the rods — not to deceive Laban, heaven forfend, but to guard himself against Laban lest he deceive him." "<b>And from the act</b> of nature in this parashah one may learn the obligation upon human beings who possess intellect and understanding to sanctify themselves at the time they mate with their wives, for behold we see in animals that the form of the offspring emerges according to the form of the thought. Therefore the Sages, of blessed memory, said: a person should always sanctify himself at the time of intercourse, and this sanctification is purity of thought — that he not think of another woman nor of other things, but only of his wife. And this is an a fortiori argument (קל וחומר) to which there is no retort: if animals, which have no understanding to comprehend the benefit or harm of a thing and act only by the force of nature, have the power to affect their offspring according to the form imprinted in their imagination and fixed in their thought — how much more so human beings, who possess great power in the rational soul to depict in their thought and in their heart both supernal and infernal things, and who have intention in intellect and understanding to direct that depiction — that they must purify their thoughts in that matter."], :book :genesis, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 30, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:17:00.837427718Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}